/// What a window manager and its console ask of the shell (211-APP-0044). The shell alone holds the operator's
/// authorities (MC-3.11); it lends a client of this interface in SLOT_SHELL to a program that asks for it
/// (REQUEST_SHELL), and a client gets only what the interface offers: the shell decides which commands it takes, and
/// asks the user in its own window before one that changes the machine.
package mind:shell@1.2.0;

interface shell {
    /// Why the shell did not do it: no window broker client, or no memory for the window; (1.1) a command it does not
    /// take from a client; the user said no in the shell's window, or did not answer; its window's session is busy
    /// with a program; (1.2) a time the clock does not take.
    enum error { unavailable, no-memory, refused, declined, busy, invalid }
    /// Opens the shell's own window, as Ctrl+Alt+F5 does, unless it is open; answers its id in the window broker, for
    /// the manager to bring it to the front.
    window: func() -> result<u32, error>;
    /// Runs one command line on the shell's authority and answers what it printed (its last 7000 bytes, UTF-8) (1.1).
    /// What system information gives any program runs at once; what changes the machine or reaches beyond it (process
    /// control, the network, a task's logs and capabilities, the system log) runs in the shell's window, opened if need
    /// be, after the user agrees there (shell/src/clients.rs). The shell's screen commands, scripts and programs are
    /// refused.
    run: func(line: string<240>) -> result<bytes<7000>, error>;
    /// Sets the clock to `date` (days since 2000-01-01) and `seconds` since midnight, without a time zone, as `date set`
    /// does, once the user agrees in the shell's window (1.2, 000-APP-0055: wm's Settings). A word call, so a caller
    /// may stop waiting while the shell asks (`mind::idl::wire::with_timeout`) and see the clock change.
    set-clock: func(date: u32, seconds: u32) -> result<_, error>;
}
